      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village reserve was named Pentridge in 1840 after surveyor Henry Boorn Foot’s wife’s birthplace in Dorset, England.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5,
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coburg%2C_Victoria",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Upfield railway line opened in 1884, and Coburg station was built in 1888, linking the suburb to Melbourne’s city centre.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coburg%2C_Victoria",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "HM Prison Pentridge, once a notorious Victorian prison, has recently been redeveloped into a residential estate, preserving its historic façade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coburg%2C_Victoria",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Coburg’s heritage includes the 19th‑century woollen mill, shoe factory, and foundry that powered the suburb’s early industrial boom.",
